 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Volumetric or 2D image data.
-**Outputs:** Compressed bitstream containing segmentation metadata and wavelet coefficients.
+**Inputs:** 2D slice data or volumetric dataset.
+**Outputs:** Compressed bitstream of wavelet coefficients. (Note: segmentation signaling overhead is typically excluded from reported bit-rates due to the lack of a suitable volumetric segmentation compression scheme [S1].)
 
 **Data Structures:**
-1.  **Segment Map:** A grid of segment IDs, one per input sample.
-2.  **Segment Metadata:** For each unique segment ID, store the chosen direction vector $d$ and contour information for coding [S1].
-3.  **Wavelet Coefficients:** Low-pass and high-pass sub-bands generated by the directional DWT [S1].
+1. **Segment Map:** A 2D grid of integer segment IDs, one per input sample.
+2. **Wavelet Coefficients:** Low-pass and high-pass sub-bands generated by the directional DWT.
 
 **Parameters:**
-*   **Kernel:** (6,6) for 2D SD-DA-DWT [S1].
-*   **Code-block size:** $64 \times 64 \times 1$ for volumetric slice-based processing to avoid inter-slice side effects [S1].
-*   **Segmentation Metric:** L1-norm of prediction (high-pass) coefficients for direction selection [S1].
+* **Kernel:** (6,6) for the 2D SD-DA-DWT decomposition [S1].
+* **Code-block size:** $64 \times 64 \times 1$ for volumetric slice-based processing to avoid inter-slice side effects [S1].
+* **Direction Selection Metric:** L1-norm of the high-pass prediction coefficients [S1].
 
 **Steps:**
-1.  **Segmentation:** Apply the Edgementation algorithm to the input data [S1].
-    *   Perform gradient-based segmentation to identify initial regions [S1].
-    *   Apply rate-distortion-driven segment merging to reduce complexity [S1].
-    *   Apply contour simplification using the algorithm from [53] to limit overhead [S1].
-2.  **Direction Selection:** For each segment, determine the optimal direction vector $d$ by comparing the L1-norm of the high-pass prediction coefficients for all candidate directions [S1].
-3.  **Transformation:** Apply the 2D SD-DA-DWT using the (6,6) kernel and the selected direction $d$ for each segment [S1].
-4.  **Coding:** Encode the resulting coefficients using EBCOT (Embedded Block Coding with Optimized Truncation) [S1].
-    *   Organize coefficients into code-blocks of size $64 \times 64 \times 1$ [S1].
-    *   Encode the segmentation map and direction vectors (overhead often ignored in theoretical evaluations due to lack of suitable volumetric segmentation compression schemes) [S1].
+1. **Segmentation:** Apply the Edgementation algorithm to the input slice [S1]. This performs gradient-based segmentation to identify initial regions, followed by rate-distortion-driven segment merging to reduce complexity, and contour simplification to limit overhead [S1]. Contour coding uses the algorithm from [53] [S1].
+2. **Direction Selection:** For each segment, determine the optimal direction vector $d$ by evaluating the L1-norm of the high-pass prediction coefficients for all candidate directions in a predefined set [S1].
+3. **Transformation:** Apply the 2D SD-DA-DWT using the (6,6) kernel and the selected direction $d$ for each segment [S1].
+4. **Coding:** Encode the resulting coefficients using EBCOT (Embedded Block Coding with Optimized Truncation) [S1]. Organize coefficients into code-blocks of size $64 \times 64 \times 1$ [S1].
+
+**Formulas:**
+The direction selection metric is the L1-norm of the high-pass coefficients $H$ within a segment $S$:
+$$ M(d) = \sum_{(i,j) \in S} |H_{i,j}(d)| $$
+where $H_{i,j}(d)$ is the high-pass prediction coefficient at spatial location $(i,j)$ for candidate direction $d$ [S1]. The optimal direction $d^*$ minimizes this metric: $d^* = \arg\min_d M(d)$ [S1].
 
